<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Legends of Panama</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> My Favorite Legend</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Create a short narrative based on a picture. Interactive: Collaborate on a group project report.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a short narrative inspired by a picture of a Panamanian legend (e.g., "Once upon a time, La India Dormida watched over the valley. After the battle, the villagers remembered her sacrifice."). Can share ideas for a group project on legends in Panama (e.g., "Let's include the legend of El Gallo Vicente."). Can contribute sentences for a group report on a Panamanian legend (e.g., "Our report is about the legend of La India Dormida.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0e6ea;">
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large picture of a well-known Panamanian legend (e.g., La India Dormida or El Gallo Vicente). Students, in pairs, briefly discuss what they remember about the legend, using simple sentences. The teacher asks a few students to share their initial thoughts orally, activating vocabulary like 'mountain', 'river', 'hero', 'fight', and simple past tense usage from previous lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models a very short, simple narrative sentence based on the picture, emphasizing past simple and past continuous, sequence words, and -ed/-ing pronunciation. For example, pointing to La India Dormida: "Once upon a time, a brave princess was watching over the valley. Suddenly, her people were fighting a terrible battle." The teacher writes these sentences on the board, highlighting "once upon a time," "was watching," "were fighting," and "suddenly."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the modeled sentences and target grammar/vocabulary.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was the princess watching *before* or *during* the battle?" (Focus on past continuous for background action)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'brave' mean? Can you give an example of a brave hero?" (Focus on vocabulary)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How do we pronounce the '-ed' in 'watched'?" (Focus on pronunciation)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5-7 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Legends of Panama".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short narrative text about "El Chupacabras" (or another relevant Panamanian legend) with approximately 5-7 sentences. The text incorporates past simple, past continuous, adverbs of time (e.g., suddenly, at night), sequence words (e.g., long ago, after), and target vocabulary (e.g., monster, creature, village, mysteriously, disappear).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Text (simplified): "Long ago, people in a small village were sleeping peacefully at night. Suddenly, a mysterious creature appeared. It was moving silently in the darkness. The villagers were very scared. One day, a brave hero decided to defend his home. After a long fight, the monster disappeared. In the end, the village was safe."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Can produce written messages and short texts about known topics to communicate information about legends.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the text silently.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What legend is this story about?" (Students write their answer on a mini-whiteboard or notebook.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read 3-4 True/False statements about the text. E.g., "The villagers were fighting when the creature appeared. (F)" "The monster disappeared in the end. (T)"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match actions to characters/time. E.g., "People were sleeping" -> "at night." "Monster appeared" -> "suddenly."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(8-10 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Past continuous (e.g., was fighting), Adverbs of time (e.g., yesterday, suddenly), Past simple to narrate events (e.g., celebrated, ended).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> legend, monster, hero, village, creature, fight, save, appear, disappear, brave, mysterious, once upon a time, suddenly, in the end.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Telling a story: "Once upon a time, the hero saved the village." Sequencing events: "First, the hero saw the witch. Then, he fought with her."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Practice pronunciation of inflectional endings -ed, /t/, /d/, or /ɪd/ in written words.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Fill-in-the-Blanks:</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from a different, short legend. They complete the blanks with the correct past simple or past continuous form of verbs, or with appropriate target vocabulary/sequence words from a word bank. Example: "Once upon a time, the people ____ (live) in a small ____ (village). One night, a ____ (mysterious/brave) ghost ____ (appear) when they ____ (sleep)." Teacher monitors and provides feedback on grammar and vocabulary choices.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled - Sentence Combining:</b> In pairs, students receive a set of two simple sentences. They must combine them using a sequence word (e.g., when, after, before) and the correct past simple/past continuous structures. Example: "The hero was exploring the forest. He saw a hidden treasure." -> "The hero was exploring the forest when he saw a hidden treasure." (Teacher ensures correct usage of time clauses and verb tenses.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Guided Narrative - Picture Sequence Writing:</b> The teacher shows a series of three pictures illustrating a new, short Panamanian legend (e.g., El Hombre Caimán). For each picture, students, individually, write one or two sentences describing the action, using past simple, past continuous, and at least one target vocabulary word or sequence adverb. Focus is on accurate sentence construction for each part of the story.</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(15-18 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Legends of Panama".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Narrative Writing from Pictures. Students work in groups of 3-4. Each group receives a set of 4-5 sequential pictures depicting a Panamanian legend (either a familiar one like "La India Dormida" or a new one like "El Pirata Cofresí"). Their task is to write a short, coherent narrative (2-3 paragraphs) based on the picture sequence. Each group member must contribute at least two sentences to the narrative, focusing on using past simple, past continuous, adverbs of time/sequence, and at least 5-7 target vocabulary words from the list (e.g., legend, hero, monster, village, ancient, river, fight, transform, appear, disappear, suddenly, in the end, once upon a time).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups analyze the pictures to understand the story's progression.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They discuss ideas, clarifying details and agreeing on key events and characters. (Sociolinguistic: Clarify details in group discussions)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students collaboratively write the narrative, with each member contributing sentences and ensuring smooth transitions between events using sequence words. (Learning Outcome: Can contribute sentences for a group report on a Panamanian legend.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, providing support, prompting language use, and encouraging peer correction within groups.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> The activity directly addresses the theme "My Favorite Legend" and scenario "Legends of Panama" by having students create their own written versions of these stories based on visual prompts, reinforcing the storytelling aspect.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(12-15 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the writing task, focusing on individual contribution and group collaboration. Students will submit their group's written narrative for evaluation using a simplified rubric.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group submits their collaborative written narrative based on the picture sequence.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of Past Simple to narrate main events (Learning Outcome).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of Past Continuous for background actions (Learning Outcome).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Inclusion of at least 5 target vocabulary words (e.g., legend, monster, hero, village, fight, transform, mysterious).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of adverbs of time and sequence markers (e.g., "Once upon a time," "suddenly," "after," "in the end") to create a coherent narrative.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and logical flow of the narrative.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Evidence of collaborative contribution (if group work).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket with the following prompts:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about writing your legend's story in English?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of your group's story are you most proud of, and why?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5-7 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using past simple and past continuous help you tell your legend's story more clearly and excitingly?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which new vocabulary words (e.g., mysterious, brave, creature) did you use effectively in your narrative today?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well during the writing activities? Did students successfully apply past simple and continuous?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson, especially regarding vocabulary recall or sentence structure for narratives? Were the picture prompts effective for generating stories?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did students collaborate and clarify details (sociolinguistic competence)?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "In the next lesson (Lesson 5 - Mediation & Integration), we will take these written narratives and prepare them for a group project presentation to the class. We will focus on summarizing and explaining information about the legends to our peers, integrating the 21st Century Skills Project."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>